window.NOTES_BLOCKS = {
 "h1.1": "\\subsection{Conditional credences are credences}",
 "b1.1.1": "\\begin{proposition}[conditional credences are credences]\\label{prop:condcred}\nSuppose $\\Cr$ satisfies the probability axioms and $\\Cr(B) > 0$. Define $\\Cr_B(A) = \\Cr(A \\mid B)$. Then $\\Cr_B$ satisfies the probability axioms.\n\\end{proposition}",
 "b1.1.2": "So everything we know about credences carries over after conditioning. There are three axioms, and so three things to check.",
 "b1.1.3": "\\begin{proof}\n\\emph{Non-negativity.} $\\Cr(A \\mid B) = \\Cr(A \\cap B) / \\Cr(B)$: a non-negative number divided by a positive one.\n\n\\emph{Normalisation.} $\\Cr(\\Omega \\mid B) = \\Cr(\\Omega \\cap B)/\\Cr(B) = \\Cr(B)/\\Cr(B) = 1$.\n\n\\emph{Finite additivity.} Take $X \\cap Y = \\varnothing$. We want $\\Cr(X \\cup Y \\mid B) = \\Cr(X \\mid B) + \\Cr(Y \\mid B)$. Starting from the left,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\Cr(X \\cup Y \\mid B)\n&= \\frac{\\Cr((X \\cup Y) \\cap B)}{\\Cr(B)} && \\text{definition} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\Cr((X \\cap B) \\cup (Y \\cap B))}{\\Cr(B)} && \\text{intersection distributes over union} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\Cr(X \\cap B) + \\Cr(Y \\cap B)}{\\Cr(B)} && \\text{finite additivity: the two pieces are disjoint} \\\\\n&= \\Cr(X \\mid B) + \\Cr(Y \\mid B). && \\text{definition, twice} \\qedhere\n\\end{align*}\n\\end{proof}",
 "b1.1.3.1": "\\emph{Non-negativity.} $\\Cr(A \\mid B) = \\Cr(A \\cap B) / \\Cr(B)$: a non-negative number divided by a positive one.",
 "b1.1.3.2": "\\emph{Normalisation.} $\\Cr(\\Omega \\mid B) = \\Cr(\\Omega \\cap B)/\\Cr(B) = \\Cr(B)/\\Cr(B) = 1$.",
 "b1.1.3.3": "\\emph{Finite additivity.} Take $X \\cap Y = \\varnothing$. We want $\\Cr(X \\cup Y \\mid B) = \\Cr(X \\mid B) + \\Cr(Y \\mid B)$. Starting from the left,",
 "b1.1.3.4": "\\begin{align*}\n\\Cr(X \\cup Y \\mid B)\n&= \\frac{\\Cr((X \\cup Y) \\cap B)}{\\Cr(B)} && \\text{definition} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\Cr((X \\cap B) \\cup (Y \\cap B))}{\\Cr(B)} && \\text{intersection distributes over union} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\Cr(X \\cap B) + \\Cr(Y \\cap B)}{\\Cr(B)} && \\text{finite additivity: the two pieces are disjoint} \\\\\n&= \\Cr(X \\mid B) + \\Cr(Y \\mid B). && \\text{definition, twice} \\qedhere\n\\end{align*}",
 "h1.2": "\\subsection{Expectation}",
 "b1.2.1": "A \\define{randomvariable}{\\emph{random variable}} $X$ is a quantity whose value is settled by which outcome occurs. It takes one of the values $x_1, \\dots, x_n$. Write $X = x_j$ for the event that it takes the value $x_j$.",
 "b1.2.2": "\\begin{definition*}[Expectation]\n\\definehere{expectation}\nWhere $x_1, \\dots, x_n$ are the possible values of $X$,\n\\[\n\\E[X] = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} x_j \\cdot \\Cr(X = x_j).\n\\]\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b1.2.3": "For a die, $\\E[X] = 1 \\cdot \\nicefrac{1}{6} + \\dots + 6 \\cdot \\nicefrac{1}{6} = 3.5$. Expected utility is the expectation of the random variable ``utility of the outcome''.",
 "b1.2.4": "\\begin{definition*}[Conditional expectation]\n\\definehere{condexpectation}\nWhere $x_1, \\dots, x_n$ are the possible values of $X$ and $\\Cr(B) > 0$,\n\\[\n\\E[X \\mid B] = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} x_j \\cdot \\Cr(X = x_j \\mid B).\n\\]\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b1.2.5": "This is the same formula, run with the conditioned credences. Those are credences, by \\propref{condcred}, so this is a genuine expectation: the expectation of $X$ under $\\Cr_B$.",
 "h1.3": "\\subsection{The law of total expectation}",
 "b1.3.1": "\\begin{proposition}[law of total expectation]\\label{prop:totalexp}\nWhere $B_1, \\dots, B_m$ partition $\\Omega$, each with $\\Cr(B_i) > 0$,\n\\[\n\\E[X] = \\sum_{i=1}^{m} \\Cr(B_i)\\,\\E[X \\mid B_i].\n\\]\n\\end{proposition}",
 "b1.3.2": "The expectation is the credence-weighted average of the conditional expectations. Compare total probability: $\\Cr(A) = \\sum_i \\Cr(B_i)\\,\\Cr(A \\mid B_i)$.",
 "b1.3.3": "For example, roll a die and let $X$ be the number shown. Split $\\Omega$ into \\emph{odd} and \\emph{even}. Then $\\E[X \\mid \\text{odd}] = \\nicefrac{1}{3}(1 + 3 + 5) = 3$, and $\\E[X \\mid \\text{even}] = \\nicefrac{1}{3}(2 + 4 + 6) = 4$. So $\\E[X] = \\Cr(\\text{odd}) \\cdot 3 + \\Cr(\\text{even}) \\cdot 4 = \\nicefrac{1}{2} \\cdot 3 + \\nicefrac{1}{2} \\cdot 4 = 3.5$.",
 "b1.3.4": "\\begin{proof}\nStart from the right-hand side.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{i} \\Cr(B_i)\\,\\E[X \\mid B_i]\n&= \\sum_{i} \\Cr(B_i) \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(X = x_j \\mid B_i) && \\text{definition of } \\E[X \\mid B_i] \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(B_i)\\,\\Cr(X = x_j \\mid B_i) && \\text{move } \\Cr(B_i) \\text{ inside} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(\\{X = x_j\\} \\cap B_i) && \\text{definition of conditional probability} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{j} x_j \\sum_{i} \\Cr(\\{X = x_j\\} \\cap B_i) && \\text{swap the sums} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(X = x_j) && \\text{total probability over the partition} \\\\\n&= \\E[X]. && \\text{definition} \\qedhere\n\\end{align*}\n\\end{proof}",
 "b1.3.4.1": "Start from the right-hand side.",
 "b1.3.4.2": "\\begin{align*}\n\\sum_{i} \\Cr(B_i)\\,\\E[X \\mid B_i]\n&= \\sum_{i} \\Cr(B_i) \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(X = x_j \\mid B_i) && \\text{definition of } \\E[X \\mid B_i] \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(B_i)\\,\\Cr(X = x_j \\mid B_i) && \\text{move } \\Cr(B_i) \\text{ inside} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{i} \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(\\{X = x_j\\} \\cap B_i) && \\text{definition of conditional probability} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{j} x_j \\sum_{i} \\Cr(\\{X = x_j\\} \\cap B_i) && \\text{swap the sums} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{j} x_j\\,\\Cr(X = x_j) && \\text{total probability over the partition} \\\\\n&= \\E[X]. && \\text{definition} \\qedhere\n\\end{align*}",
 "h1": "\\section{Three things about probability}",
 "h2.1": "\\subsection{What utility is}",
 "b2.1.1": "Many formulas so far have had a $U$ in them. We never said what it is.",
 "b2.1.2": "\\emph{Utility as pleasure}: an outcome has high utility to the extent that it increases pleasure and reduces pain. Put into MEU: rational agents maximise expected pleasure.",
 "b2.1.3": "\\emph{Utility as value}: an outcome has utility to the extent that it is actually valuable, whether in the prudential, moral, or another sense.\nMEU then becomes MEV: Maximise Expected Value.",
 "b2.1.4": "\\emph{Utility as degree of desire}: how much the agent wants the outcome, all things considered, whatever it is she wants.",
 "b2.1.5": "\\emph{Utility as a pure representative device}: utility need not have any psychological connection with the agent, and is merely a mathematical representation of preferences the agent already has.",
 "b2.1.6": "Words can mean what we want them to, but I generally use utility to refer to the representative device.\nThis is the sense we will mostly be concerned with here.",
 "h2.2": "\\subsection{Correcting some misconceptions (BDRC)}",
 "b2.2.1": "\\textbf{Misconception 1.} BDRC, on the MEP Principle (``rational agents maximize their expected degree of pleasure''):",
 "b2.2.2": "\\begin{quote}\n``The MEP Principle is a form of psychological hedonism. Psychological hedonism is the view that the only thing that ultimately motivates people is their own pleasure and pain.''\n\\end{quote}",
 "b2.2.3": "False. Psychological hedonism is indeed a thesis about what ultimately motivates people. This makes it independent of the MEP Principle. As stated, the MEP Principle is a thesis about what people should do: what people do \\emph{insofar as they are rational}. The MEP Principle could be false and psychological hedonism true; or the MEP Principle true and psychological hedonism false.",
 "b2.2.4": "\\textbf{Misconception 2.} BDRC:",
 "b2.2.5": "\\begin{quote}\n``The utility of a[n] outcome measures the extent to which the agent in question wants the outcome to obtain.'' (BDRC, p.~76)\n\\end{quote}",
 "b2.2.6": "Schwarz is fixing which sense of ``utility'' he will use: degree of desire. Fine as a stipulation. But it is not our sense, and it is not what expected utility theory delivers.",
 "b2.2.7": "If utility represents preferences in the way we will see this week, then utility does align with what the agent wants (prefers), in the sense that outcomes or prospects with higher utility are preferred to ones with lower utility. But that is the only sense in which utility measures what the agent wants. There is no requirement that utility match up with the extent to which an agent wants the outcome or prospect. What utility matches up with is the agent's attitudes to risk. One outcome having relatively high utility compared to another means that the agent will prefer even relatively small probabilities of the higher-utility outcome. That's all it means.",
 "h2.3": "\\subsection{Some more about rationality: what do we mean?}",
 "b2.3.1": "Rationality, at minimum, seems to demand a kind of coherence between our attitudes, preferences, and actions. If we prefer $A$ to $B$, and we are offered a choice between $A$ and $B$, and nothing else is at stake, it seems irrational to choose $B$. If we believe that it is raining and also believe that it is not raining, that seems irrational too.",
 "b2.3.2": "Rationality might be more substantive than this. Schwarz suggests one way it might be: if rational egoism were true, so that it were irrational ever to do anything that was worse for us. But this is not the only way. It could be that we're required to care about certain things, like love or friendship or pleasure or carrot cake. It could be that we're always rationally required to do the morally right thing (the moral overridingness thesis). It could be that rationality requires us to go off into the woods somewhere to commune with god.",
 "b2.3.3": "We don't need to assume that rationality is or is not substantive. The assumptions we're making about rationality are deliberately quite minimal, and neutral between different visions of what rationality requires --- minimal or not.",
 "h2.4": "\\subsection{What we are going to show}",
 "b2.4.1": "We start from the agent's preferences over gambles, not from her utilities. We assume the preferences satisfy some structural principles: the axioms. We then show that there is a utility function $u$ on outcomes such that she prefers one gamble to another exactly when it has higher expected utility, and that $u$ is unique up to unit and zero. This is a representation theorem: the numbers are read off the preferences.",
 "b2.4.2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\n\t\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, every node/.style={transform shape}]\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=white, minimum width=3.2cm] (g) at (0.2,2.0) {$\\succsim$ over gambles};\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=white, minimum width=3.2cm] (p) at (5.4,2.0) {$\\succsim$ over prospects};\n\t\t\\draw[ar] (g) -- node[lab, above] {stochastic\\\\equivalence} (p);\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=white, minimum width=3.4cm] (ax) at (2.2,-0.2) {satisfies the\\\\four axioms};\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=white, minimum width=3.4cm] (rep) at (8.6,-0.2) {is represented by\\\\expected utility};\n\t\t\\draw[{Latex[length=2mm]}-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick] (ax) -- node[lab, above] {the theorem} (rep);\n\t\t\\draw[gray] (p.south) -- (5.4,0.5);\n\t\\end{tikzpicture}\n\n\\caption{The road. Two steps: gambles to prospects, then the theorem: the axioms hold of $\\succsim$ over prospects if and only if $\\succsim$ is represented by a utility function.}\n\\label{fig:road}\n\\end{figure}",
 "h2": "\\section{Preference and utility}",
 "h3.1": "\\subsection{Notation}",
 "b3.1.1": "We write $A \\succsim B$ when the agent \\define{weakpref}{\\emph{weakly prefers}} $A$ to $B$: $A$ is at least as good as $B$, by her lights. $A \\succ B$ is \\define{strictpref}{\\emph{strict preference}}: $A \\succsim B$ and not $B \\succsim A$. $A \\sim B$ is \\define{indifference}{\\emph{indifference}}: $A \\succsim B$ and $B \\succsim A$. So $\\succsim$ is the basic notion; the other two are defined from it.",
 "h3.2": "\\subsection{Preference and choice}",
 "b3.2.1": "If $A \\succ B$ and the choice is between $A$ and $B$, the agent \\emph{will} choose $A$, and \\emph{will not} choose $B$. If $B \\succ A$, the agent will choose $B$, and will not choose $A$. If $A \\sim B$, either: choice can't tell them apart.",
 "b3.2.2": "We won't commit to what preference is. Two things matter: it is linked to choice as above, and it is open to rational evaluation. It is defined over every pair of gambles, not just the choices the agent actually faces.",
 "h3.3": "\\subsection{What gets ranked}",
 "b3.3.1": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n             & \\textbf{Heads} ($\\nicefrac{1}{2}$) & \\textbf{Tails} ($\\nicefrac{1}{2}$) \\\\ \\midrule\nTake the bet & $\\$100$ & $\\$0$   \\\\\nDecline      & $\\$50$  & $\\$50$  \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b3.3.2": "A \\define{gamble}{\\emph{gamble}} is an act whose outcome depends on the state. Each row of a decision table is one. $\\succsim$ ranges over all gambles, including ones nobody has offered.",
 "h3.4": "\\subsection{The ordering principles}",
 "b3.4.1": "\\begin{principle}[Reflexivity]\n\\definehere{reflexivity}\n$A \\succsim A$, for every $A$.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b3.4.2": "\\begin{principle}[Transitivity]\n\\definehere{transitivity}\nIf $A \\succsim B$ and $B \\succsim C$, then $A \\succsim C$.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b3.4.3": "Both are among the assumptions of expected utility theory.",
 "b3.4.4": "\\begin{qbox}{Exercises: the preference order}\n\\q{Show that if $A \\succ B$ and $B \\succ C$, then $A \\succ C$.}\n  {From $A \\succsim B$ and $B \\succsim C$, transitivity gives $A \\succsim C$. Suppose also that $C \\succsim A$. Then $C \\succsim A$ and $A \\succsim B$ give $C \\succsim B$ by transitivity, which contradicts $B \\succ C$. So not $C \\succsim A$, and hence $A \\succ C$.}\n\\q{Show that if $A \\sim B$ and $B \\succ C$, then $A \\succ C$.}\n  {From $A \\succsim B$ and $B \\succsim C$, transitivity gives $A \\succsim C$. Suppose also that $C \\succsim A$. Then $C \\succsim A$ and $A \\succsim B$ give $C \\succsim B$ by transitivity, which contradicts $B \\succ C$. So not $C \\succsim A$, and hence $A \\succ C$.}\n\\q{Show that $A \\succ B$ and $B \\succ A$ cannot both hold.}\n  {$A \\succ B$ says, in part, that not $B \\succsim A$. But $B \\succ A$ says, in part, that $B \\succsim A$. So they cannot both hold.}\n\\end{qbox}",
 "b3.4.5": "Each follows from the definitions plus transitivity. We'll use facts like these without comment.",
 "h3": "\\section{Preferences}",
 "b4.0.1": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n    & \\textbf{Heads} ($\\nicefrac{1}{2}$) & \\textbf{Tails} ($\\nicefrac{1}{2}$) \\\\ \\midrule\n$G$ & $a$ & $b$ \\\\\n$G'$ & $b$ & $a$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b4.0.2": "$G$ and $G'$ are different gambles, with the same chance of every final outcome.",
 "b4.0.3": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (2.0,3.5) {$G$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r1) at (0.4,2.2) {1};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,3.0) {$a$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.4) {$b$};\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (9.0,3.5) {$G'$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r2) at (7.4,2.2) {2};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,3.0) {$b$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,1.4) {$a$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Different gambles. The same chance of every final outcome.}\n\\label{fig:twogambles}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (2.0,3.5) {$G$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r1) at (0.4,2.2) {1};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,3.0) {$a$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.4) {$b$};\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (9.0,3.5) {$G'$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r2) at (7.4,2.2) {2};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,3.0) {$b$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,1.4) {$a$};\n\t\\node[font=\\large] at (5.4,2.2) {$\\sim$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Stochastic equivalence: the agent is indifferent between them.}\n\\label{fig:twogamblessim}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b4.0.3-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (2.0,3.5) {$G$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r1) at (0.4,2.2) {1};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,3.0) {$a$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.4) {$b$};\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (9.0,3.5) {$G'$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r2) at (7.4,2.2) {2};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,3.0) {$b$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,1.4) {$a$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Different gambles. The same chance of every final outcome.}\n\\label{fig:twogambles}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b4.0.3-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (2.0,3.5) {$G$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r1) at (0.4,2.2) {1};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,3.0) {$a$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.4) {$b$};\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (9.0,3.5) {$G'$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r2) at (7.4,2.2) {2};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,3.0) node[lpr, pos=0.4, above] {Heads} node[lpr, pos=0.85, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (10.0,1.4) node[lpr, pos=0.4, below] {Tails} node[lpr, pos=0.85, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,3.0) {$b$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (10.1,1.4) {$a$};\n\t\\node[font=\\large] at (5.4,2.2) {$\\sim$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Stochastic equivalence: the agent is indifferent between them.}\n\\label{fig:twogamblessim}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b4.0.4": "\\begin{principle}[Stochastic equivalence]\n\\definehere{stochasticequivalence}\nIf two gambles give every final outcome the same probability, then the agent is indifferent between them.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b4.0.5": "Only the chances of the final outcomes matter, not which states carry them. Stochastic equivalence is what lets us talk about the chances of outcomes instead of the states.",
 "b4.0.6": "It can be doubted. The doubts mostly arise when preferences have gaps --- when Completeness fails. See Hare, ``Take the Sugar'', \\emph{Analysis} 70 (2010); Francis and Gustafsson, ``Stochastic Dominance for Incomplete Preferences'', \\emph{Analysis} 86 (2026), 299--311.",
 "h4": "\\section{Stochastic equivalence}",
 "h5.1": "\\subsection{Prospects}",
 "b5.1.1": "Fix the set of final outcomes. There may be infinitely many. A \\define{prospect}{\\emph{prospect}} $P$ assigns a probability $P(x)$ to each outcome $x$, positive for only finitely many of them; they sum to $1$. Finitely many, because a decision problem has finitely many outcomes in play. The preferences themselves range over infinitely many prospects.",
 "b5.1.2": "\\begin{definition*}[The prospect of a gamble]\n\\definehere{prospectof}\nThe prospect of a gamble $G$, written $P_G$, is the function taking each outcome $x$ to the probability that $G$ results in $x$, computed from your credences in the states.\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b5.1.3": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n     & \\textbf{Heads} ($\\nicefrac{1}{2}$) & \\textbf{Tails} ($\\nicefrac{1}{2}$) \\\\ \\midrule\n$G$  & $a$ & $b$ \\\\\n$G'$ & $b$ & $a$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\qquad\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}l@{}}\n$P_G(a) = \\nicefrac{1}{2}$, \\ $P_G(b) = \\nicefrac{1}{2}$ \\\\[0.3em]\n$P_{G'}(a) = \\nicefrac{1}{2}$, \\ $P_{G'}(b) = \\nicefrac{1}{2}$\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b5.1.4": "Here $P_G = P_{G'}$: the same prospect. Stochastic equivalence says exactly that $G \\sim G'$.",
 "h5.2": "\\subsection{From gambles to prospects}",
 "b5.2.1": "\\begin{proposition}[from gambles to prospects]\\label{prop:reduced}\nSuppose $\\succsim$ satisfies stochastic equivalence and transitivity. If $P_G = P_{G'}$ and $P_H = P_{H'}$, then $G \\succsim H$ iff $G' \\succsim H'$.\n\\end{proposition}",
 "b5.2.2": "\\begin{proof}\nBy stochastic equivalence $G' \\sim G$ and $H \\sim H'$. Suppose $G \\succsim H$. Then $G' \\succsim G \\succsim H$, so $G' \\succsim H$ by transitivity; and $G' \\succsim H \\succsim H'$, so $G' \\succsim H'$. The other direction is the same argument with the primes swapped.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b5.2.2.1": "By stochastic equivalence $G' \\sim G$ and $H \\sim H'$. Suppose $G \\succsim H$. Then $G' \\succsim G \\succsim H$, so $G' \\succsim H$ by transitivity; and $G' \\succsim H \\succsim H'$, so $G' \\succsim H'$. The other direction is the same argument with the primes swapped.",
 "b5.2.3": "\\begin{definition*}[Preference over prospects]\n\\definehere{prefprospects}\nFor prospects $P$ and $Q$: $P \\succsim Q$ iff $G \\succsim H$ for some gambles $G$, $H$ with $P_G = P$ and $P_H = Q$.\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b5.2.4": "This is well defined, by \\propref{reduced}: any gambles with those prospects give the same answer. And $G \\succsim H$ iff $P_G \\succsim P_H$: the preferences over gambles are completely fixed by the preferences over prospects.",
 "b5.2.5": "Assumed, not proved here: if the ordering properties hold of $\\succsim$ over gambles then, given stochastic equivalence, they hold of $\\succsim$ over prospects. Also in the background: every prospect is the prospect of \\emph{some} gamble.",
 "h5.3": "\\subsection{Mixing}",
 "b5.3.1": "\\begin{definition*}[Mixing]\n\\definehere{mixing}\nFor prospects $A$ and $B$ and $p \\in [0,1]$, $\\mix{A}{p}{B}$ is the prospect with\n\\[\n(\\mix{A}{p}{B})(x) = p \\cdot A(x) + (1-p) \\cdot B(x) \\qquad \\text{for each outcome } x.\n\\]\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b5.3.2": "That is: ``$A$ with probability $p$, otherwise $B$.'' $A$ and $B$ need not be single outcomes. They can themselves be prospects.",
 "b5.3.3": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (3.0,3.0) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (3.0,1.0) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,3.0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.0) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{A}{p}{B}$, where $A$ and $B$ are prospects.}\n\\label{fig:mixingone}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (3.0,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (b) at (3.0,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (b) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{3}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{2}{3}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,3.6) {$x_1$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,2.4) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,1.6) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,0.4) {$x_3$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,3.5) {$A$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,0.5) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Open up $A$ and $B$: each is itself a spread of chances over outcomes.}\n\\label{fig:mixingtwo}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (3.0,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (b) at (3.0,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (b) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{3}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{2}{3}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,3.6) {$x_1$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,2.4) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,1.6) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,0.4) {$x_3$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,3.5) {$A$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,0.5) {$B$};\n\t\\node[font=\\large] at (7.0,2.0) {$=$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (s) at (7.7,2.0) {4};\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.3,3.3);\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.3,2.0);\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.3,0.7);\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.4,3.3) {$x_1$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$\\nicefrac{p}{2}$}};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.4,2.0) {$x_2$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$\\nicefrac{p}{2} + \\nicefrac{(1-p)}{3}$}};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.4,0.7) {$x_3$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$\\nicefrac{2(1-p)}{3}$}};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Multiply through, and it is one prospect over the outcomes.}\n\\label{fig:mixing}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b5.3.3-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (3.0,3.0) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (3.0,1.0) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,3.0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.0) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{A}{p}{B}$, where $A$ and $B$ are prospects.}\n\\label{fig:mixingone}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b5.3.3-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (3.0,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (b) at (3.0,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (b) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{3}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{2}{3}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,3.6) {$x_1$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,2.4) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,1.6) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,0.4) {$x_3$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,3.5) {$A$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,0.5) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Open up $A$ and $B$: each is itself a spread of chances over outcomes.}\n\\label{fig:mixingtwo}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b5.3.3-f3": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (3.0,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (b) at (3.0,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (b) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (5.4,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{1}{2}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$\\nicefrac{1}{3}$};\n\t\\draw (b) -- (5.4,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$\\nicefrac{2}{3}$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,3.6) {$x_1$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,2.4) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,1.6) {$x_2$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (5.5,0.4) {$x_3$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,3.5) {$A$};\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (3.0,0.5) {$B$};\n\t\\node[font=\\large] at (7.0,2.0) {$=$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (s) at (7.7,2.0) {4};\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.3,3.3);\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.3,2.0);\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.3,0.7);\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.4,3.3) {$x_1$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$\\nicefrac{p}{2}$}};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.4,2.0) {$x_2$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$\\nicefrac{p}{2} + \\nicefrac{(1-p)}{3}$}};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.4,0.7) {$x_3$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$\\nicefrac{2(1-p)}{3}$}};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Multiply through, and it is one prospect over the outcomes.}\n\\label{fig:mixing}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b5.3.4": "This is an equation, not a preference. Prospects with the same chances \\emph{are} the same prospect.",
 "h5.4": "\\subsection{Four facts about mixing}",
 "b5.4.1": "\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item[(M1)] $\\mix{A}{p}{A} = A$.\n\t\\item[(M2)] $\\mix{A}{p}{B} = \\mix{B}{(1-p)}{A}$.\n\t\\item[(M3)] $\\mix{(\\mix{A}{p}{B})}{q}{B} = \\mix{A}{(pq)}{B}$.\n\t\\item[(M4)] $\\mix{A}{p}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} = \\mix{A}{[p + (1-p)q]}{B}$.\n\\end{itemize}",
 "b5.4.2": "All four are arithmetic: compute the chance of each outcome on both sides. The proof leans on (M1) and (M4).",
 "h5.5": "\\subsection{The mixture space}",
 "b5.5.1": "The set of all prospects over the outcomes, together with the mixing operation, is a \\define{mixturespace}{\\emph{mixture space}}. From here on, $\\succsim$ is a relation over prospects. This is justified by the Stochastic Equivalence assumption. Single outcomes can be identified with prospects too: $x$ with the prospect that gives $x$ for certain.",
 "h5.6": "\\subsection{What we are going to prove}",
 "b5.6.1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\n\t\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, every node/.style={transform shape}]\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=gray!30, minimum width=3.2cm] (g) at (0.2,2.0) {$\\succsim$ over gambles};\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=gray!30, minimum width=3.2cm] (p) at (5.4,2.0) {$\\succsim$ over prospects};\n\t\t\\draw[ar] (g) -- node[lab, above] {stochastic\\\\equivalence} (p);\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=white, minimum width=3.4cm] (ax) at (2.2,-0.2) {satisfies the\\\\four axioms};\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=white, minimum width=3.4cm] (rep) at (8.6,-0.2) {is represented by\\\\expected utility};\n\t\t\\draw[{Latex[length=2mm]}-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick] (ax) -- node[lab, above] {the theorem} (rep);\n\t\t\\draw[gray] (p.south) -- (5.4,0.5);\n\t\\end{tikzpicture}\n\n\\caption{The first leg is done; the theorem is the second row.}\n\\label{fig:roadhalf}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b5.6.2": "The first leg is done: $\\succsim$ now ranges over prospects. The theorem says that $\\succsim$ satisfies the axioms \\emph{if and only if} it is \\emph{represented} by expected utility, for some $u$ on outcomes. \\define{represented}{\\emph{Represented}} means: $P \\succsim Q$ iff $\\EU(P) \\geq \\EU(Q)$, for every pair of prospects. The ranking by $\\succsim$ and the ranking by expected utility are the same ranking. Two axioms so far: reflexivity and transitivity. Three to come.",
 "h5": "\\section{Prospects}",
 "h6.1": "\\subsection{Completeness}",
 "b6.1.1": "\\begin{principle}[Completeness]\n\\definehere{completeness}\nFor all prospects $A$ and $B$: $A \\succsim B$ or $B \\succsim A$.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b6.1.2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (1.5,3.9) {Without it};\n\t\\node[ldot] (t) at (1.5,2.8) {};\n\t\\node[ldot] (l) at (0.5,1.6) {};\n\t\\node[ldot] (rr) at (2.5,1.6) {};\n\t\\node[ldot] (bt) at (1.5,0.4) {};\n\t\\draw[ar] (l) -- (t);\n\t\\draw[ar] (rr) -- (t);\n\t\\draw[ar] (bt) -- (l);\n\t\\draw[ar] (bt) -- (rr);\n\t\\draw[dashed, gray] (l) -- node[above, font=\\scriptsize, gray] {?} (rr);\n\t\\node[anchor=east, font=\\small] at (0.3,1.6) {$B$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west, font=\\small] at (2.7,1.6) {$C$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west, font=\\small] at (1.7,2.9) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=north, font=\\small] at (1.5,0.2) {$D$};\n\t\\node[font=\\small] at (8.0,3.9) {With it};\n\t\\draw[ar] (6.0,0.4) -- (10.4,0.4);\n\t\\foreach \\x/\\l in {6.5/D, 7.6/C, 8.4/B, 9.8/A} {\n\t\t\\node[ldot] at (\\x,0.4) {};\n\t\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (\\x,0.4) {$\\l$};\n\t}\n\t\\node[font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (10.8,0.4) {better};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Without Completeness a pair can be incomparable; with it (and transitivity) everything lines up.}\n\\label{fig:completeness}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b6.1.3": "No gaps: every pair is comparable. With transitivity, everything lines up.",
 "h6.2": "\\subsection{Independence}",
 "b6.2.1": "\\begin{principle}[Independence]\n\\definehere{independence}\nFor all prospects $A$, $B$, $C$ and every $p \\in (0,1)$: $A \\succsim B$ iff $\\mix{A}{p}{C} \\succsim \\mix{B}{p}{C}$ iff $\\mix{C}{p}{A} \\succsim \\mix{C}{p}{B}$.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b6.2.2": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n                & $E$ \\ ($\\Cr(E) = p$) & $\\neg E$ \\ ($1-p$) \\\\ \\midrule\n$\\mix{A}{p}{C}$ & $A$ & $C$ \\\\\n$\\mix{B}{p}{C}$ & $B$ & $C$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b6.2.3": "One way to realise these prospects is with gambles that agree if $\\neg E$. But any gambles with these prospects will do; they need not agree anywhere. The principle is a biconditional, in weak preference, and both directions matter. Apply it twice: $\\mix{A}{p}{C} \\succsim \\mix{B}{p}{C}$ iff $A \\succsim B$ iff $\\mix{A}{q}{C} \\succsim \\mix{B}{q}{C}$. So the weight makes no difference.",
 "b6.2.4": "\\begin{principle}[Sure-thing principle]\n\\definehere{surething}\nGambles $G$, $G'$, $H$, $H'$ and an event $E$. If $G$, $H$ agree if $E$ and so do $G'$, $H'$, while $G$, $G'$ agree if $\\neg E$ and so do $H$, $H'$: then $G \\succsim G'$ iff $H \\succsim H'$.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b6.2.5": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n     & $E$ \\ ($p$) & $\\neg E$ \\ ($1-p$) \\\\ \\midrule\n$G$  & $A$ & $C$ \\\\\n$G'$ & $B$ & $C$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\qquad\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}r|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n     & $E$ \\ ($p$) & $\\neg E$ \\ ($1-p$) \\\\ \\midrule\n$H$  & $A$ & $D$ \\\\\n$H'$ & $B$ & $D$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\\\[0.5em]\n$G \\succsim G'$ \\quad iff \\quad $H \\succsim H'$\n\\end{center}",
 "b6.2.6": "Take two gambles that agree on what happens if $\\neg E$, and replace the shared outcome $C$ by another shared outcome $D$. The sure-thing principle says that the two comparisons come out the same way. It is a principle about \\emph{states}: what the two gambles share cannot affect the comparison. Independence implies it, and with stochastic equivalence the two come to the same thing, at least when there are finitely many states.",
 "h6.3": "\\subsection{Continuity}",
 "b6.3.1": "\\begin{principle}[Continuity]\n\\definehere{continuity}\nIf $A \\succ B \\succ C$, then there is some $p \\in (0,1)$ with $B \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{C}$.\n\\end{principle}",
 "b6.3.2": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$B$ sits strictly between $A$ and $C$.}\n\\label{fig:continuityone}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (2.6,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small, gray] at (2.6,0.9) {small $p$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (2.6,0.75) -- (2.6,0.15);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{A}{p}{C}$ runs from $C$ (at $p = 0$) to $A$ (at $p = 1$).}\n\\label{fig:continuitytwo}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (4.2,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small, gray] at (4.2,0.9) {larger $p$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (4.2,0.75) -- (4.2,0.15);\n\t\\draw[gray, -{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (3.1,0.9) -- (3.8,0.9);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Somewhere on the way it must draw level with $B$.}\n\\label{fig:continuitythree}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (6.2,0.9) {$\\mix{A}{p}{C} \\sim B$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (6.2,0.75) -- (6.2,0.15);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{That $p$ is what Continuity promises.}\n\\label{fig:continuity}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b6.3.2-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$B$ sits strictly between $A$ and $C$.}\n\\label{fig:continuityone}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b6.3.2-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (2.6,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small, gray] at (2.6,0.9) {small $p$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (2.6,0.75) -- (2.6,0.15);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{A}{p}{C}$ runs from $C$ (at $p = 0$) to $A$ (at $p = 1$).}\n\\label{fig:continuitytwo}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b6.3.2-f3": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (4.2,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small, gray] at (4.2,0.9) {larger $p$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (4.2,0.75) -- (4.2,0.15);\n\t\\draw[gray, -{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (3.1,0.9) -- (3.8,0.9);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Somewhere on the way it must draw level with $B$.}\n\\label{fig:continuitythree}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b6.3.2-f4": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$C$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (6.2,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.2,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (6.2,0.9) {$\\mix{A}{p}{C} \\sim B$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (6.2,0.75) -- (6.2,0.15);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{That $p$ is what Continuity promises.}\n\\label{fig:continuity}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b6.3.3": "So no prospect is \\emph{infinitely} better than another. When Continuity fails: an agent ranks $b \\succ c \\succ a$ but prefers $c$ to every mixture of $b$ and $a$, however heavily weighted towards $b$. Then there is no $p$, and no number to assign to $c$. The question is whether such preferences could be rational.",
 "h6.4": "\\subsection{The axioms}",
 "b6.4.1": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\definehere{axioms}Transitivity & $A \\succsim B$ and $B \\succsim C$ give $A \\succsim C$ \\\\\nCompleteness & $A \\succsim B$ or $B \\succsim A$ \\\\\nIndependence & $A \\succsim B$ iff $\\mix{A}{p}{C} \\succsim \\mix{B}{p}{C}$ iff $\\mix{C}{p}{A} \\succsim \\mix{C}{p}{B}$ \\\\\nContinuity   & $A \\succ B \\succ C$ gives some $p$ with $B \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{C}$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b6.4.2": "Reflexivity follows from Completeness (take $B$ to be $A$), so it is dropped. Four axioms. Stochastic equivalence sits behind all of them: it is why $\\succsim$ ranges over prospects at all.",
 "h6": "\\section{The remaining axioms}",
 "b7.0.1": "\\begin{theorem}[Expected Utility Theorem]\\label{thm:eut}\n\\definehere{eut}\nLet $\\succsim$ be a preference relation over prospects.\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\t\\item[(i)] $\\succsim$ satisfies the four axioms if and only if some function $u$ on outcomes represents it by expected utility: for all prospects $P$ and $Q$,\n\t\\[\n\tP \\succsim Q \\quad\\text{iff}\\quad \\EU(P) \\geq \\EU(Q), \\qquad\\text{where } \\EU(P) = \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u(x),\n\t\\]\n\tthe sum running over the finitely many outcomes $P$ gives positive probability to.\n\t\\item[(ii)] Any two such functions $u$ and $u'$ differ only in unit and zero: $u' = a \\cdot u + c$ for some $a > 0$ and some $c$.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\\end{theorem}",
 "h7.1": "\\subsection{The idea of the proof}",
 "b7.1.1": "Pick two prospects, one better than the other: $A_1 \\succ A_0$. The choice is more or less arbitrary. Assign $u(A_1) = 1$ and $u(A_0) = 0$: the unit and the zero. For any $B$ between them, there is exactly one $p$ with $B \\sim \\mix{A_1}{p}{A_0}$. Assign $u(B) = p$.",
 "b7.1.2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (1.0,0) {$u = 0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (10.4,0) {$u = 1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.8,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (6.8,0.9) {$B$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (6.8,0.75) -- (6.8,0.15);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.8,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt, font=\\small] at (6.8,0) {$\\mix{A_1}{p}{A_0}$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (6.8,0) {$u(B) = p$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The unit and the zero, and a prospect $B$ between them.}\n\\label{fig:idea}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.1.3": "If $B \\succ A_1$, there is exactly one $p$ with $A_1 \\sim \\mix{B}{p}{A_0}$; assign $u(B) = \\nicefrac{1}{p}$. If $A_0 \\succ B$, there is exactly one $p$ with $A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{p}{B}$; assign $u(B) = -\\nicefrac{p}{(1-p)}$.",
 "b7.1.4": "The rest of the proof fills in the details, and shows that this assignment represents the \\emph{whole} of the agent's preferences: the ranking by $u$ is the ranking by $\\succsim$, and $u$ respects mixing. The other direction: preferences that violate any one of the axioms cannot be represented by a utility function.",
 "h7.2": "\\subsection{Beginning the proof}",
 "b7.2.1": "The proof follows Fishburn, \\emph{The Foundations of Expected Utility} (1982), ch.\\ 2, Theorem 1. The same proof is Theorem 8.4 of his \\emph{Utility Theory for Decision Making} (1970).",
 "b7.2.2": "If no prospect is strictly preferred to any other, set $u$ constant. Done. Otherwise fix two prospects $A_1 \\succ A_0$: the \\emph{unit} and the \\emph{zero}.",
 "h7.3": "\\subsection{Step 1: more of the better thing is better}",
 "b7.3.1": "\\begin{lemma}[more of the better thing is better]\\label{lem:monotone}\nIf $A \\succ B$ and $p, q \\in [0,1]$, then $\\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}$ iff $p > q$.\n\\end{lemma}",
 "b7.3.2": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The ends.}\n\\label{fig:lemmaoneA}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (4.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (4.2,0) {$\\mix{A}{q}{B}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{A}{q}{B}$.}\n\\label{fig:lemmaoneB}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (4.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (4.2,0) {$\\mix{A}{q}{B}$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.6,0) {};\n\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (7.6,0) {$\\mix{A}{p}{B}$};\n\t\\draw[gray, -{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (4.2,-1.0) -- node[below, font=\\scriptsize, gray] {more weight on $A$} (7.6,-1.0);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Sliding weight from $B$ onto $A$ moves you up.}\n\\label{fig:lemma1}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b7.3.2-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The ends.}\n\\label{fig:lemmaoneA}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.3.2-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (4.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (4.2,0) {$\\mix{A}{q}{B}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{A}{q}{B}$.}\n\\label{fig:lemmaoneB}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.3.2-f3": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (4.2,0) {};\n\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (4.2,0) {$\\mix{A}{q}{B}$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.6,0) {};\n\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (7.6,0) {$\\mix{A}{p}{B}$};\n\t\\draw[gray, -{Latex[length=1.6mm]}] (4.2,-1.0) -- node[below, font=\\scriptsize, gray] {more weight on $A$} (7.6,-1.0);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Sliding weight from $B$ onto $A$ moves you up.}\n\\label{fig:lemma1}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.3.3": "\\begin{proof}\nGiven $A \\succ B$ and $0 < q < p < 1$ (the edge cases $q = 0$ and $p = 1$ take one step each). Write $s = \\nicefrac{(p-q)}{(1-q)}$, so that $s + (1-s)q = p$.\n\\begin{align*}\nA &\\succ B && \\text{the hypothesis} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{q}{A} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{Independence, mixing $A$ in front of both sides at weight $q$} \\\\\nA &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{(M1) on the left} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{s}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} &\\succ \\mix{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})}{s}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} && \\text{Independence again, at weight $s$} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{s}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{(M1) on the right} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{[s + (1-s)q]}{B} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{(M4) on the left, with $s$ in the role of $p$} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{p}{B} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}. && \\text{$s + (1-s)q = p$, by the choice of $s$}\n\\end{align*}\nThat is one direction. For the other: if $p = q$ the two sides are the same prospect, and if $p < q$ the argument just given puts $\\mix{A}{q}{B}$ strictly above $\\mix{A}{p}{B}$. Either way, not $\\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}$.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.3.3.1": "Given $A \\succ B$ and $0 < q < p < 1$ (the edge cases $q = 0$ and $p = 1$ take one step each). Write $s = \\nicefrac{(p-q)}{(1-q)}$, so that $s + (1-s)q = p$.",
 "b7.3.3.2": "\\begin{align*}\nA &\\succ B && \\text{the hypothesis} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{q}{A} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{Independence, mixing $A$ in front of both sides at weight $q$} \\\\\nA &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{(M1) on the left} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{s}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} &\\succ \\mix{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})}{s}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} && \\text{Independence again, at weight $s$} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{s}{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{(M1) on the right} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{[s + (1-s)q]}{B} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{(M4) on the left, with $s$ in the role of $p$} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{p}{B} &\\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}. && \\text{$s + (1-s)q = p$, by the choice of $s$}\n\\end{align*}",
 "b7.3.3.3": "That is one direction. For the other: if $p = q$ the two sides are the same prospect, and if $p < q$ the argument just given puts $\\mix{A}{q}{B}$ strictly above $\\mix{A}{p}{B}$. Either way, not $\\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}$.",
 "h7.4": "\\subsection{Step 2: a mixture of worse (or equal) prospects is worse (or equal)}",
 "b7.4.1": "\\begin{lemma}[a mixture of worse (or equal) prospects is worse (or equal)]\\label{lem:between}\nIf $A \\succsim X$ and $A \\succsim Y$, then $A \\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y}$.\n\\end{lemma}",
 "b7.4.2": "\\begin{proof}\n\\begin{align*}\nA &\\succsim X && \\text{the first hypothesis} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{p}{Y} &\\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y} && \\text{Independence, mixing both sides with $Y$ at weight $p$} \\\\\nA &\\succsim Y && \\text{the second hypothesis} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{p}{A} &\\succsim \\mix{A}{p}{Y} && \\text{Independence, mixing $A$ in front of both sides} \\\\\nA &\\succsim \\mix{A}{p}{Y} && \\text{(M1) on the left} \\\\\nA &\\succsim \\mix{A}{p}{Y} \\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y} && \\text{the two results together} \\\\\nA &\\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y}. && \\text{transitivity}\n\\end{align*}\nA mixture of prospects that $A$ is at least as good as is itself one that $A$ is at least as good as. The same proof with the preferences reversed gives the mirror image.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.4.2.1": "\\begin{align*}\nA &\\succsim X && \\text{the first hypothesis} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{p}{Y} &\\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y} && \\text{Independence, mixing both sides with $Y$ at weight $p$} \\\\\nA &\\succsim Y && \\text{the second hypothesis} \\\\\n\\mix{A}{p}{A} &\\succsim \\mix{A}{p}{Y} && \\text{Independence, mixing $A$ in front of both sides} \\\\\nA &\\succsim \\mix{A}{p}{Y} && \\text{(M1) on the left} \\\\\nA &\\succsim \\mix{A}{p}{Y} \\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y} && \\text{the two results together} \\\\\nA &\\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y}. && \\text{transitivity}\n\\end{align*}",
 "b7.4.2.2": "A mixture of prospects that $A$ is at least as good as is itself one that $A$ is at least as good as. The same proof with the preferences reversed gives the mirror image.",
 "b7.4.3": "\\begin{definition*}[Interval]\n\\definehere{interval}\nFor prospects $A \\succsim B$, the \\emph{interval} $[B, A]$ is the set of prospects $X$ with $A \\succsim X \\succsim B$.\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b7.4.4": "By \\lemref{between} and its mirror image, an interval is \\emph{closed under mixing}: if $X$ and $Y$ are in $[B, A]$, so is $\\mix{X}{q}{Y}$. Any \\emph{finitely many} prospects have a best and a worst among them, by Completeness and Transitivity. So they all lie in one interval.",
 "b7.4.5": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\foreach \\x/\\l in {1.6/Y, 3.4/A_0, 5.9/Z, 7.7/A_1, 9.6/X} {\n\t\t\\node[ldot] at (\\x,0) {};\n\t\t\\node[above=3pt, font=\\small] at (\\x,0) {$\\l$};\n\t}\n\t\\draw[decorate, decoration={brace, mirror, amplitude=5pt}, thick] (1.6,-0.35) -- (9.6,-0.35);\n\t\\node[below=8pt, font=\\small] at (5.6,-0.35) {$[B, A]$, with $B$ the worst of them and $A$ the best};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Finitely many prospects always lie in one interval.}\n\\label{fig:interval}\n\\end{figure}",
 "h7.5": "\\subsection{Step 3: calibration on an interval}",
 "b7.5.1": "\\begin{lemma}[calibration on an interval]\\label{lem:calibrate}\nFix $A \\succ B$. For every $X$ in $[B, A]$ there is exactly one $p \\in [0,1]$ with $X \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{B}$.\n\\end{lemma}",
 "b7.5.2": "\\begin{proof}\n\\emph{Existence.} If $X \\sim A$, take $p = 1$; if $X \\sim B$, take $p = 0$. Otherwise $A \\succ X \\succ B$, and Continuity supplies a $p$.\n\n\\emph{Uniqueness.} Two different values $p > q$ would give $\\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}$ by \\lemref{monotone}, but both are $\\sim X$: a contradiction, by transitivity.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.5.2.1": "\\emph{Existence.} If $X \\sim A$, take $p = 1$; if $X \\sim B$, take $p = 0$. Otherwise $A \\succ X \\succ B$, and Continuity supplies a $p$.",
 "b7.5.2.2": "\\emph{Uniqueness.} Two different values $p > q$ would give $\\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}$ by \\lemref{monotone}, but both are $\\sim X$: a contradiction, by transitivity.",
 "b7.5.3": "\\begin{definition*}\n\\definehere{calibration}\nFor $X$ in $[B, A]$, $u_{AB}(X)$ is the unique $p$ with $X \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{B}$.\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b7.5.4": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (1.0,0) {$B$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (1.0,0) {$u_{AB} = 0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (10.4,0) {$A$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (10.4,0) {$u_{AB} = 1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.8,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (6.8,0.9) {$X$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (6.8,0.75) -- (6.8,0.15);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (6.8,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt, font=\\small] at (6.8,0) {$\\mix{A}{p}{B}$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (6.8,0) {$u_{AB}(X) = p$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Calibration on $[B, A]$: $X$ is placed at the weight $p$ of the $A$--$B$ mixture it is indifferent to.}\n\\label{fig:calibrate}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.5.5": "There is one calibration per interval; the subscript keeps track of which ends were used. $u_{AB}(A) = 1$ and $u_{AB}(B) = 0$, by (M1).",
 "h7.6": "\\subsection{Step 4: $u_{AB}$ represents $\\succsim$ on the interval}",
 "b7.6.1": "\\begin{lemma}[$u_{AB}$ represents $\\succsim$ on the interval]\\label{lem:represents}\nFor $X$ and $Y$ in $[B, A]$: $X \\succsim Y$ iff $u_{AB}(X) \\geq u_{AB}(Y)$.\n\\end{lemma}",
 "b7.6.2": "\\begin{proof}\nWrite $p = u_{AB}(X)$ and $q = u_{AB}(Y)$, so that $X \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{B}$ and $Y \\sim \\mix{A}{q}{B}$.\n\\begin{align*}\nX \\succsim Y \\quad &\\text{iff} \\quad \\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succsim \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{transitivity, in both directions} \\\\\n&\\text{iff} \\quad \\text{not } \\mix{A}{q}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{p}{B} && \\text{Completeness} \\\\\n&\\text{iff} \\quad \\text{not } q > p && \\text{\\lemref{monotone}, in both directions} \\\\\n&\\text{iff} \\quad p \\geq q,\n\\end{align*}\nwhich is $u_{AB}(X) \\geq u_{AB}(Y)$. Every step was an iff.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.6.2.1": "Write $p = u_{AB}(X)$ and $q = u_{AB}(Y)$, so that $X \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{B}$ and $Y \\sim \\mix{A}{q}{B}$.",
 "b7.6.2.2": "\\begin{align*}\nX \\succsim Y \\quad &\\text{iff} \\quad \\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succsim \\mix{A}{q}{B} && \\text{transitivity, in both directions} \\\\\n&\\text{iff} \\quad \\text{not } \\mix{A}{q}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{p}{B} && \\text{Completeness} \\\\\n&\\text{iff} \\quad \\text{not } q > p && \\text{\\lemref{monotone}, in both directions} \\\\\n&\\text{iff} \\quad p \\geq q,\n\\end{align*}",
 "b7.6.2.3": "which is $u_{AB}(X) \\geq u_{AB}(Y)$. Every step was an iff.",
 "h7.7": "\\subsection{Step 5: $u_{AB}$ respects mixing on the interval}",
 "b7.7.1": "\\begin{lemma}[$u_{AB}$ respects mixing on the interval]\\label{lem:linear}\nFor $X$ and $Y$ in $[B, A]$ and $p \\in [0,1]$: $u_{AB}(\\mix{X}{p}{Y}) = p \\cdot u_{AB}(X) + (1-p) \\cdot u_{AB}(Y)$.\n\\end{lemma}",
 "b7.7.2": "\\begin{proof}\nThe mixture $\\mix{X}{p}{Y}$ is in $[B, A]$ by \\lemref{between}, so $u_{AB}$ is defined on it. Write $q = u_{AB}(X)$ and $r = u_{AB}(Y)$, so that $X \\sim \\mix{A}{q}{B}$ and $Y \\sim \\mix{A}{r}{B}$.\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mix{X}{p}{Y} &\\sim \\mix{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})}{p}{Y} && \\text{Independence, mixing $Y$ behind both at weight $p$} \\\\\n&\\sim \\mix{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})}{p}{(\\mix{A}{r}{B})} && \\text{Independence, on the other component} \\\\\n&= \\mix{A}{[pq + (1-p)r]}{B} && \\text{arithmetic: the weights are averaged}\n\\end{align*}\nSo $u_{AB}(\\mix{X}{p}{Y}) = pq + (1-p)r$ by \\lemref{calibrate}: that is the unique calibrating value.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.7.2.1": "The mixture $\\mix{X}{p}{Y}$ is in $[B, A]$ by \\lemref{between}, so $u_{AB}$ is defined on it. Write $q = u_{AB}(X)$ and $r = u_{AB}(Y)$, so that $X \\sim \\mix{A}{q}{B}$ and $Y \\sim \\mix{A}{r}{B}$.",
 "b7.7.2.2": "\\begin{align*}\n\\mix{X}{p}{Y} &\\sim \\mix{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})}{p}{Y} && \\text{Independence, mixing $Y$ behind both at weight $p$} \\\\\n&\\sim \\mix{(\\mix{A}{q}{B})}{p}{(\\mix{A}{r}{B})} && \\text{Independence, on the other component} \\\\\n&= \\mix{A}{[pq + (1-p)r]}{B} && \\text{arithmetic: the weights are averaged}\n\\end{align*}",
 "b7.7.2.3": "So $u_{AB}(\\mix{X}{p}{Y}) = pq + (1-p)r$ by \\lemref{calibrate}: that is the unique calibrating value.",
 "b7.7.3": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (2.6,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (c) at (2.6,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (c) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$q$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-q$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$r$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-r$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,3.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,2.4) {$B$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,1.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,0.4) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{X}{p}{Y}$, with $X$ and $Y$ replaced by their calibrations.}\n\\label{fig:lemmafiveA}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (2.6,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (c) at (2.6,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (c) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$q$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-q$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$r$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-r$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,3.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,2.4) {$B$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,1.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,0.4) {$B$};\n\t\\node[font=\\large] at (6.6,2.0) {$=$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (s) at (7.4,2.0) {4};\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.0,3.0);\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.0,1.0);\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.1,3.0) {$A$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$pq + (1-p)r$}};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.1,1.0) {$B$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$1 - pq - (1-p)r$}};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Multiply through: $A$ with total weight $pq + (1-p)r$, $B$ with the rest.}\n\\label{fig:lemma5}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b7.7.3-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (2.6,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (c) at (2.6,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (c) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$q$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-q$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$r$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-r$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,3.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,2.4) {$B$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,1.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,0.4) {$B$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{$\\mix{X}{p}{Y}$, with $X$ and $Y$ replaced by their calibrations.}\n\\label{fig:lemmafiveA}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.7.3-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[cnode] (r) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (a) at (2.6,3.0) {2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (c) at (2.6,1.0) {3};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (a) node[lpr, midway, above] {$p$};\n\t\\draw (r) -- (c) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-p$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,3.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$q$};\n\t\\draw (a) -- (4.8,2.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-q$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,1.6) node[lpr, midway, above] {$r$};\n\t\\draw (c) -- (4.8,0.4) node[lpr, midway, below] {$1-r$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,3.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,2.4) {$B$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,1.6) {$A$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (4.9,0.4) {$B$};\n\t\\node[font=\\large] at (6.6,2.0) {$=$};\n\t\\node[cnode] (s) at (7.4,2.0) {4};\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.0,3.0);\n\t\\draw (s) -- (9.0,1.0);\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.1,3.0) {$A$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$pq + (1-p)r$}};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.1,1.0) {$B$ \\ {\\scriptsize\\color{gray}$1 - pq - (1-p)r$}};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Multiply through: $A$ with total weight $pq + (1-p)r$, $B$ with the rest.}\n\\label{fig:lemma5}\n\\end{figure}",
 "h7.8": "\\subsection{Rescaling keeps both properties}",
 "b7.8.1": "\\begin{proposition}[rescaling keeps both properties]\\label{prop:rescaling}\nSuppose $g$ represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing on some interval. Take $a > 0$ and any $c$, and put $h = a \\cdot g + c$. Then $h$ represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing on that interval.\n\\end{proposition}",
 "b7.8.2": "\\begin{proof}\n\\emph{$h$ represents $\\succsim$.} Since $a > 0$: $h(X) \\geq h(Y)$ iff $a\\,g(X) + c \\geq a\\,g(Y) + c$ iff $g(X) \\geq g(Y)$ iff $X \\succsim Y$.\n\n\\emph{$h$ respects mixing.} Using $c = p\\,c + (1-p)\\,c$:\n\\begin{align*}\nh(\\mix{X}{p}{Y}) &= a\\,[p\\,g(X) + (1-p)\\,g(Y)] + c \\\\\n&= p\\,[a\\,g(X) + c] + (1-p)\\,[a\\,g(Y) + c] \\\\\n&= p\\,h(X) + (1-p)\\,h(Y). \\qedhere\n\\end{align*}\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.8.2.1": "\\emph{$h$ represents $\\succsim$.} Since $a > 0$: $h(X) \\geq h(Y)$ iff $a\\,g(X) + c \\geq a\\,g(Y) + c$ iff $g(X) \\geq g(Y)$ iff $X \\succsim Y$.",
 "b7.8.2.2": "\\emph{$h$ respects mixing.} Using $c = p\\,c + (1-p)\\,c$:",
 "b7.8.2.3": "\\begin{align*}\nh(\\mix{X}{p}{Y}) &= a\\,[p\\,g(X) + (1-p)\\,g(Y)] + c \\\\\n&= p\\,[a\\,g(X) + c] + (1-p)\\,[a\\,g(Y) + c] \\\\\n&= p\\,h(X) + (1-p)\\,h(Y). \\qedhere\n\\end{align*}",
 "b7.8.3": "So a rescaling with a positive factor loses nothing. We use this twice: next, and for uniqueness.",
 "h7.9": "\\subsection{The five lemmas}",
 "b7.9.1": "\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item \\lemref{monotone}: if $A \\succ B$ and $p, q \\in [0,1]$, then $\\mix{A}{p}{B} \\succ \\mix{A}{q}{B}$ iff $p > q$.\n\t\\item \\lemref{between}: if $A \\succsim X$ and $A \\succsim Y$, then $A \\succsim \\mix{X}{p}{Y}$; likewise with $\\succsim$ reversed. So an interval is closed under mixing.\n\t\\item \\lemref{calibrate}: fix $A \\succ B$. For every $X$ in $[B, A]$ there is exactly one $p \\in [0,1]$ with $X \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{B}$. That $p$ is $u_{AB}(X)$.\n\t\\item \\lemref{represents}: for $X$ and $Y$ in $[B, A]$, $X \\succsim Y$ iff $u_{AB}(X) \\geq u_{AB}(Y)$.\n\t\\item \\lemref{linear}: for $X$ and $Y$ in $[B, A]$, $u_{AB}(\\mix{X}{p}{Y}) = p \\cdot u_{AB}(X) + (1-p) \\cdot u_{AB}(Y)$.\n\\end{itemize}",
 "h7.10": "\\subsection{Step 6: beyond the interval}",
 "b7.10.1": "Take any prospect $Z$. Let $A$ be the best of $Z$, $A_1$, $A_0$ and $B$ the worst, so $[B, A]$ contains all three. Calibrate on that interval, then \\emph{rescale} so that $A_1$ scores $1$ and $A_0$ scores $0$:",
 "b7.10.2": "\\begin{definition*}\n\\[\nf(X) = \\frac{u_{AB}(X) - u_{AB}(A_0)}{u_{AB}(A_1) - u_{AB}(A_0)} \\qquad \\text{for } X \\text{ in } [B, A].\n\\]\n\\end{definition*}",
 "b7.10.3": "The denominator is positive, since $A_1 \\succ A_0$ and $u_{AB}$ represents $\\succsim$. $f$ is a rescaling of $u_{AB}$ with a positive factor, so it still represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing on $[B, A]$: \\lemref{represents} and \\lemref{linear}, with \\propref{rescaling}. Now three cases show what $f(Z)$ must be --- without reference to the interval.",
 "b7.10.4": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (3.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (3.4,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (3.4,0) {$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (7.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (7.4,0) {$1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (5.6,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (5.6,0.9) {$Z \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\beta}{A_0}$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (5.6,0.75) -- (5.6,0.15);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Case 1: $A_1 \\succsim Z \\succsim A_0$. \\lemref{calibrate}: $Z \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\beta}{A_0}$, for exactly one $\\beta$.}\n\\label{fig:caseone}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (3.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (3.4,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (3.4,0) {$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (7.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (7.4,0) {$1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.0,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (10.0,0.9) {$Z$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (10.0,0.75) -- (10.0,0.15);\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (7.4,0.9) {$A_1 \\sim \\mix{Z}{\\gamma}{A_0}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Case 2: $Z \\succ A_1$. \\lemref{calibrate} on $[A_0, Z]$: $A_1 \\sim \\mix{Z}{\\gamma}{A_0}$, for exactly one $\\gamma$.}\n\\label{fig:casetwo}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (3.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (3.4,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (3.4,0) {$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (7.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (7.4,0) {$1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (1.0,0.9) {$Z$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (1.0,0.75) -- (1.0,0.15);\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (3.9,0.9) {$A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\alpha}{Z}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Case 3: $A_0 \\succ Z$. \\lemref{calibrate} on $[Z, A_1]$: $A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\alpha}{Z}$, for exactly one $\\alpha$.}\n\\label{fig:casethree}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b7.10.4-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (3.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (3.4,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (3.4,0) {$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (7.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (7.4,0) {$1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (5.6,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (5.6,0.9) {$Z \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\beta}{A_0}$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (5.6,0.75) -- (5.6,0.15);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Case 1: $A_1 \\succsim Z \\succsim A_0$. \\lemref{calibrate}: $Z \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\beta}{A_0}$, for exactly one $\\beta$.}\n\\label{fig:caseone}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.10.4-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (3.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (3.4,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (3.4,0) {$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (7.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (7.4,0) {$1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10.0,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (10.0,0.9) {$Z$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (10.0,0.75) -- (10.0,0.15);\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (7.4,0.9) {$A_1 \\sim \\mix{Z}{\\gamma}{A_0}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Case 2: $Z \\succ A_1$. \\lemref{calibrate} on $[A_0, Z]$: $A_1 \\sim \\mix{Z}{\\gamma}{A_0}$, for exactly one $\\gamma$.}\n\\label{fig:casetwo}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.10.4-f3": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.4,0);\n\t\\node[ldot] at (3.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (3.4,0) {$A_0$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (3.4,0) {$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (7.4,0) {};\n\t\\node[below=3pt] at (7.4,0) {$A_1$};\n\t\\node[below=17pt, font=\\small, gray] at (7.4,0) {$1$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (1.0,0.9) {};\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (1.0,0.9) {$Z$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (1.0,0.75) -- (1.0,0.15);\n\t\\node[above=2pt, font=\\small] at (3.9,0.9) {$A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\alpha}{Z}$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Case 3: $A_0 \\succ Z$. \\lemref{calibrate} on $[Z, A_1]$: $A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\alpha}{Z}$, for exactly one $\\alpha$.}\n\\label{fig:casethree}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.10.5": "In Case 1, $f(Z) = \\beta \\cdot 1 + (1 - \\beta) \\cdot 0 = \\beta$. In Case 2, $1 = f(A_1) = \\gamma\\,f(Z) + (1 - \\gamma) \\cdot 0$, so $f(Z) = \\nicefrac{1}{\\gamma}$; and $\\gamma > 0$, since $A_1 \\succ A_0$. In Case 3, $0 = f(A_0) = \\alpha \\cdot 1 + (1 - \\alpha)\\,f(Z)$, so $f(Z) = -\\nicefrac{\\alpha}{(1 - \\alpha)}$; and $\\alpha < 1$, likewise.",
 "b7.10.6": "In each case, $f(Z)$ is fixed by an indifference that mentions only $Z$, $A_1$ and $A_0$. The interval played no part. So two intervals containing $Z$, $A_1$, $A_0$ give the same $f(Z)$. Define $u(Z)$ to be that common value. This is exactly the assignment from the idea of the proof: $\\beta$ in between, $\\nicefrac{1}{\\gamma}$ above, $-\\nicefrac{\\alpha}{(1-\\alpha)}$ below.",
 "b7.10.7": "$u$ is now defined on every prospect. Take any $X$ and $Y$, and an interval $[B, A]$ containing $X$, $Y$, $A_1$, $A_0$. On that interval $u$ is the rescaled calibration $f$, which represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing there. So $X \\succsim Y$ iff $u(X) \\geq u(Y)$, and $u(\\mix{X}{q}{Y}) = q\\,u(X) + (1-q)\\,u(Y)$.",
 "h7.11": "\\subsection{Step 7: $u$ is expected utility}",
 "b7.11.1": "A prospect $X$ gives positive probability to finitely many outcomes, say $x_1, \\dots, x_n$, with probabilities $p_1, \\dots, p_n$. It is a mixture of them, taken one at a time: $X = \\mix{x_1}{p_1}{X'}$, where $X'$ is the rest of $X$, rescaled to sum to $1$. And $X'$ is a mixture in the same way. Since $u$ respects mixing, applying this $n - 1$ times gives\n\\[\nu(X) = p_1\\,u(x_1) + p_2\\,u(x_2) + \\dots + p_n\\,u(x_n) = \\EU(X).\n\\]\nSince $u$ represents $\\succsim$: $X \\succsim Y$ iff $\\EU(X) \\geq \\EU(Y)$. That is the ``only if'' half of Theorem~\\ref{thm:eut}(i).",
 "h7.12": "\\subsection{Step 8: uniqueness}",
 "b7.12.1": "\\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\\ref{thm:eut}(ii)]\nSuppose $v$ also represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing. We want $a > 0$ and $c$ with $v = a \\cdot u + c$.\n\n\\emph{Which $a$ and $c$?} Only one choice is possible. At $A_0$: $v(A_0) = a \\cdot u(A_0) + c = c$. At $A_1$: $v(A_1) = a \\cdot u(A_1) + c = a + c$. So $c = v(A_0)$ and $a = v(A_1) - v(A_0)$.\n\n\\emph{The candidate.} Put $w = a \\cdot u + c$ with those values. By construction, $w(A_1) = v(A_1)$ and $w(A_0) = v(A_0)$. And $a > 0$, since $v$ represents $\\succsim$ and $A_1 \\succ A_0$. So $w$ is a positive rescaling of $u$: by \\propref{rescaling}, it represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing.\n\n\\emph{What is left:} show $v(Z) = w(Z)$ for every $Z$. Suppose not, for some $Z$. Three cases, each a contradiction. Throughout, $v$ and $w$ both represent $\\succsim$ (so indifferent prospects get equal values), both respect mixing, and they agree at $A_1$ and $A_0$.\n\n\\emph{Case 1:} $A_1 \\succsim Z \\succsim A_0$, so $Z \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\beta}{A_0}$. Both respect mixing, and they agree at the two ends:\n\\[\nv(Z) = \\beta\\,v(A_1) + (1-\\beta)\\,v(A_0) = \\beta\\,w(A_1) + (1-\\beta)\\,w(A_0) = w(Z).\n\\]\n\n\\emph{Case 2:} $Z \\succ A_1$, so $A_1 \\sim \\mix{Z}{\\gamma}{A_0}$, with $\\gamma > 0$. Read the indifference through each function, then compare:\n\\[\n\\gamma\\,v(Z) + (1-\\gamma)\\,v(A_0) = v(A_1) = w(A_1) = \\gamma\\,w(Z) + (1-\\gamma)\\,w(A_0).\n\\]\nCancel and divide by $\\gamma > 0$: $v(Z) = w(Z)$.\n\n\\emph{Case 3:} $A_0 \\succ Z$, so $A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\alpha}{Z}$, with $\\alpha < 1$. The same, at the other end:\n\\[\n\\alpha\\,v(A_1) + (1-\\alpha)\\,v(Z) = v(A_0) = w(A_0) = \\alpha\\,w(A_1) + (1-\\alpha)\\,w(Z).\n\\]\nCancel and divide by $1 - \\alpha > 0$: $v(Z) = w(Z)$.\n\nContradiction in every case. So $v = w = a \\cdot u + c$, with $a = v(A_1) - v(A_0) > 0$ and $c = v(A_0)$.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.12.1.1": "Suppose $v$ also represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing. We want $a > 0$ and $c$ with $v = a \\cdot u + c$.",
 "b7.12.1.2": "\\emph{Which $a$ and $c$?} Only one choice is possible. At $A_0$: $v(A_0) = a \\cdot u(A_0) + c = c$. At $A_1$: $v(A_1) = a \\cdot u(A_1) + c = a + c$. So $c = v(A_0)$ and $a = v(A_1) - v(A_0)$.",
 "b7.12.1.3": "\\emph{The candidate.} Put $w = a \\cdot u + c$ with those values. By construction, $w(A_1) = v(A_1)$ and $w(A_0) = v(A_0)$. And $a > 0$, since $v$ represents $\\succsim$ and $A_1 \\succ A_0$. So $w$ is a positive rescaling of $u$: by \\propref{rescaling}, it represents $\\succsim$ and respects mixing.",
 "b7.12.1.4": "\\emph{What is left:} show $v(Z) = w(Z)$ for every $Z$. Suppose not, for some $Z$. Three cases, each a contradiction. Throughout, $v$ and $w$ both represent $\\succsim$ (so indifferent prospects get equal values), both respect mixing, and they agree at $A_1$ and $A_0$.",
 "b7.12.1.5": "\\emph{Case 1:} $A_1 \\succsim Z \\succsim A_0$, so $Z \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\beta}{A_0}$. Both respect mixing, and they agree at the two ends:\n\\[\nv(Z) = \\beta\\,v(A_1) + (1-\\beta)\\,v(A_0) = \\beta\\,w(A_1) + (1-\\beta)\\,w(A_0) = w(Z).\n\\]",
 "b7.12.1.6": "\\emph{Case 2:} $Z \\succ A_1$, so $A_1 \\sim \\mix{Z}{\\gamma}{A_0}$, with $\\gamma > 0$. Read the indifference through each function, then compare:\n\\[\n\\gamma\\,v(Z) + (1-\\gamma)\\,v(A_0) = v(A_1) = w(A_1) = \\gamma\\,w(Z) + (1-\\gamma)\\,w(A_0).\n\\]\nCancel and divide by $\\gamma > 0$: $v(Z) = w(Z)$.",
 "b7.12.1.7": "\\emph{Case 3:} $A_0 \\succ Z$, so $A_0 \\sim \\mix{A_1}{\\alpha}{Z}$, with $\\alpha < 1$. The same, at the other end:\n\\[\n\\alpha\\,v(A_1) + (1-\\alpha)\\,v(Z) = v(A_0) = w(A_0) = \\alpha\\,w(A_1) + (1-\\alpha)\\,w(Z).\n\\]\nCancel and divide by $1 - \\alpha > 0$: $v(Z) = w(Z)$.",
 "b7.12.1.8": "Contradiction in every case. So $v = w = a \\cdot u + c$, with $a = v(A_1) - v(A_0) > 0$ and $c = v(A_0)$.",
 "b7.12.2": "\\begin{proposition}[rescaled expected utility is expected utility]\\label{prop:rescaledeu}\nFor any $a > 0$ and $c$, with $u' = a \\cdot u + c$: $\\EU'(P) = a \\cdot \\EU(P) + c$, so $\\EU'(P) \\geq \\EU'(Q)$ exactly when $\\EU(P) \\geq \\EU(Q)$.\n\\end{proposition}",
 "b7.12.3": "\\begin{proof}\n\\begin{align*}\n\\EU'(P) &= \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u'(x) && \\text{expected utility computed with $u'$} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,[a\\,u(x) + c] && \\text{substitute $u' = a\\,u + c$} \\\\\n&= a \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u(x) + c \\sum_{x} P(x) && \\text{collect the $a$ terms and the $c$ terms} \\\\\n&= a \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u(x) + c \\cdot 1 && \\text{the probabilities sum to $1$} \\\\\n&= a \\cdot \\EU(P) + c. && \\text{an increasing linear function of $\\EU$}\n\\end{align*}\nSince $a > 0$, $\\EU'(P) \\geq \\EU'(Q)$ exactly when $\\EU(P) \\geq \\EU(Q)$.\n\\end{proof}",
 "b7.12.3.1": "\\begin{align*}\n\\EU'(P) &= \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u'(x) && \\text{expected utility computed with $u'$} \\\\\n&= \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,[a\\,u(x) + c] && \\text{substitute $u' = a\\,u + c$} \\\\\n&= a \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u(x) + c \\sum_{x} P(x) && \\text{collect the $a$ terms and the $c$ terms} \\\\\n&= a \\sum_{x} P(x)\\,u(x) + c \\cdot 1 && \\text{the probabilities sum to $1$} \\\\\n&= a \\cdot \\EU(P) + c. && \\text{an increasing linear function of $\\EU$}\n\\end{align*}",
 "b7.12.3.2": "Since $a > 0$, $\\EU'(P) \\geq \\EU'(Q)$ exactly when $\\EU(P) \\geq \\EU(Q)$.",
 "h7.13": "\\subsection{Step 9: the other direction}",
 "b7.13.1": "Suppose $\\succsim$ is represented by expected utility for some $u$. Then each axiom holds.",
 "b7.13.2": "\\emph{Transitivity and Completeness.} $\\geq$ on numbers is transitive and complete, and $\\succsim$ copies it: $P \\succsim Q$ iff $\\EU(P) \\geq \\EU(Q)$.",
 "b7.13.3": "\\emph{Independence.} $\\EU(\\mix{A}{p}{C}) = p\\,\\EU(A) + (1-p)\\,\\EU(C)$, so\n\\[\n\\EU(\\mix{A}{p}{C}) \\geq \\EU(\\mix{B}{p}{C}) \\quad\\text{iff}\\quad p\\,\\EU(A) \\geq p\\,\\EU(B) \\quad\\text{iff}\\quad \\EU(A) \\geq \\EU(B),\n\\]\nsince $p > 0$. The same for $\\mix{C}{p}{A}$ and $\\mix{C}{p}{B}$, since $1 - p > 0$.",
 "b7.13.4": "\\emph{Continuity.} Given $\\EU(A) > \\EU(B) > \\EU(C)$, put $p = \\nicefrac{[\\EU(B) - \\EU(C)]}{[\\EU(A) - \\EU(C)]}$, which lies in $(0,1)$. Then\n\\[\n\\EU(\\mix{A}{p}{C}) = \\EU(C) + p\\,[\\EU(A) - \\EU(C)] = \\EU(B),\n\\]\nso $B \\sim \\mix{A}{p}{C}$.",
 "b7.13.5": "Both directions of (i), and (ii). The theorem is proved.",
 "b7.13.6": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\n\t\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9, every node/.style={transform shape}]\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=gray!30, minimum width=3.2cm] (g) at (0.2,2.0) {$\\succsim$ over gambles};\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=gray!30, minimum width=3.2cm] (p) at (5.4,2.0) {$\\succsim$ over prospects};\n\t\t\\draw[ar] (g) -- node[lab, above] {stochastic\\\\equivalence} (p);\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=gray!30, minimum width=3.4cm] (ax) at (2.2,-0.2) {satisfies the\\\\four axioms};\n\t\t\\node[box, fill=gray!30, minimum width=3.4cm] (rep) at (8.6,-0.2) {is represented by\\\\expected utility};\n\t\t\\draw[{Latex[length=2mm]}-{Latex[length=2mm]}, thick] (ax) -- node[lab, above] {the theorem} (rep);\n\t\t\\draw[gray] (p.south) -- (5.4,0.5);\n\t\\end{tikzpicture}\n\n\\caption{Done.}\n\\label{fig:roaddone}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b7.13.7": "What we have shown: four axioms on preference hold exactly when some utility function on outcomes represents it --- however many outcomes there are. Preference between prospects is exactly comparison of expected utility. The function is fixed once the unit and zero are: an interval scale.",
 "h7": "\\section{The Expected Utility Theorem}",
 "b8.0.1": "\\emph{Representation, not explanation.} $u$ was built out of the preferences. The theorem says the preferences can be described as expected-utility maximising. It does not say the agent computes anything, or that $u$ is a quantity in her head that causes the preferences.",
 "b8.0.2": "\\emph{What rationality requires, on this view.} Not ``calculate expectations and choose the biggest''; rather, have preferences satisfying the axioms. Maximising expected utility is a consequence, not an instruction.",
 "b8.0.3": "\\emph{How many utility functions?} Some $u$ exists; the theorem picks out no particular one. Every $a \\cdot u + c$ with $a > 0$ does the same job, and nothing else does. Ratios of \\emph{differences} do mean something: they survive every rescaling.",
 "h8.1": "\\subsection{Risk aversion, two senses}",
 "b8.1.1": "\\begin{figseq}{}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.0,0) node[right, font=\\scriptsize] {money};\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[below right, font=\\scriptsize] {$u$};\n\t\\draw[thick] plot[domain=0:10, samples=40] (\\x, {5.4*sqrt(\\x/10)});\n\t\\node[above left, font=\\scriptsize] at (8.8,5.07) {$u$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (0,-0.1) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10,5.4) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (10,-0.1) {$\\$100$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (10,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The black curve is $u$, the utility of money. A fair coin for $\\$100$ or $\\$0$, against $\\$50$ for sure.}\n\\label{fig:concaveA}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.0,0) node[right, font=\\scriptsize] {money};\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[below right, font=\\scriptsize] {$u$};\n\t\\draw[thick] plot[domain=0:10, samples=40] (\\x, {5.4*sqrt(\\x/10)});\n\t\\node[above left, font=\\scriptsize] at (8.8,5.07) {$u$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (0,-0.1) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10,5.4) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (10,-0.1) {$\\$100$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (10,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\draw[gray] (0,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\node[below right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (7.2,3.89) {line};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (5,2.7) {};\n\t\\node[right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (5.1,2.5) {$\\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$0) + \\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$100)$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (5,0) -- (5,2.7);\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (5,-0.1) {$\\$50$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The grey line joins $u(\\$0)$ to $u(\\$100)$. Its midpoint is the gamble's expected utility.}\n\\label{fig:concaveB}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.0,0) node[right, font=\\scriptsize] {money};\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[below right, font=\\scriptsize] {$u$};\n\t\\draw[thick] plot[domain=0:10, samples=40] (\\x, {5.4*sqrt(\\x/10)});\n\t\\node[above left, font=\\scriptsize] at (8.8,5.07) {$u$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (0,-0.1) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10,5.4) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (10,-0.1) {$\\$100$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (10,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\draw[gray] (0,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\node[below right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (7.2,3.89) {line};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (5,2.7) {};\n\t\\node[right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (5.1,2.5) {$\\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$0) + \\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$100)$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (5,0) -- (5,2.7);\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (5,-0.1) {$\\$50$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (5,3.818) {};\n\t\\node[left, font=\\scriptsize] at (4.9,4.0) {$u(\\$50)$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (5,2.7) -- (5,3.818);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{A concave $u$ puts $u(\\$50)$ \\emph{above} the line: the sure thing wins. Risk aversion about money, inside EUT.}\n\\label{fig:concave}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\end{figseq}",
 "b8.1.1-f1": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.0,0) node[right, font=\\scriptsize] {money};\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[below right, font=\\scriptsize] {$u$};\n\t\\draw[thick] plot[domain=0:10, samples=40] (\\x, {5.4*sqrt(\\x/10)});\n\t\\node[above left, font=\\scriptsize] at (8.8,5.07) {$u$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (0,-0.1) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10,5.4) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (10,-0.1) {$\\$100$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (10,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The black curve is $u$, the utility of money. A fair coin for $\\$100$ or $\\$0$, against $\\$50$ for sure.}\n\\label{fig:concaveA}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b8.1.1-f2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.0,0) node[right, font=\\scriptsize] {money};\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[below right, font=\\scriptsize] {$u$};\n\t\\draw[thick] plot[domain=0:10, samples=40] (\\x, {5.4*sqrt(\\x/10)});\n\t\\node[above left, font=\\scriptsize] at (8.8,5.07) {$u$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (0,-0.1) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10,5.4) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (10,-0.1) {$\\$100$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (10,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\draw[gray] (0,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\node[below right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (7.2,3.89) {line};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (5,2.7) {};\n\t\\node[right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (5.1,2.5) {$\\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$0) + \\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$100)$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (5,0) -- (5,2.7);\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (5,-0.1) {$\\$50$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{The grey line joins $u(\\$0)$ to $u(\\$100)$. Its midpoint is the gamble's expected utility.}\n\\label{fig:concaveB}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b8.1.1-f3": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (11.0,0) node[right, font=\\scriptsize] {money};\n\t\\draw[-{Latex[length=2mm]}] (0,0) -- (0,6.0) node[below right, font=\\scriptsize] {$u$};\n\t\\draw[thick] plot[domain=0:10, samples=40] (\\x, {5.4*sqrt(\\x/10)});\n\t\\node[above left, font=\\scriptsize] at (8.8,5.07) {$u$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (0,0) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (0,-0.1) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (10,5.4) {};\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (10,-0.1) {$\\$100$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (10,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\draw[gray] (0,0) -- (10,5.4);\n\t\\node[below right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (7.2,3.89) {line};\n\t\\node[ldot, gray] at (5,2.7) {};\n\t\\node[right, font=\\scriptsize, gray] at (5.1,2.5) {$\\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$0) + \\tfrac{1}{2}u(\\$100)$};\n\t\\draw[gray, dashed] (5,0) -- (5,2.7);\n\t\\node[below, font=\\scriptsize] at (5,-0.1) {$\\$50$};\n\t\\node[ldot] at (5,3.818) {};\n\t\\node[left, font=\\scriptsize] at (4.9,4.0) {$u(\\$50)$};\n\t\\draw[dashed] (5,2.7) -- (5,3.818);\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{A concave $u$ puts $u(\\$50)$ \\emph{above} the line: the sure thing wins. Risk aversion about money, inside EUT.}\n\\label{fig:concave}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b8.1.2": "Risk aversion about money is preferring $\\$50$ for sure to a fair coin for $\\$100$ or $\\$0$. This is compatible with EUT: a concave $u$ does it. Risk aversion about utility itself --- preferring a sure thing to a gamble with the same expected utility --- is ruled out.",
 "h8": "\\section{What the theorem means}",
 "b9.0.1": "\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item \\textbf{Completeness.} Must every pair be comparable?\n\t\\item \\textbf{Continuity.} Is nothing infinitely better than anything else?\n\t\\item \\textbf{Transitivity.} Next week.\n\t\\item \\textbf{Independence.} Now: the Allais paradox.\n\\end{itemize}",
 "h9.1": "\\subsection{The Allais paradox}",
 "b9.1.1": "Option 1: $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ for certain. Option 2: $\\$5{,}000{,}000$ with probability $0.10$, $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ with $0.89$, $\\$0$ with $0.01$.",
 "b9.1.2": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\bfseries] at (1.6,3.5) {Option 1};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r1) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,2.0) node[lpr, midway, above] {$1$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,2.0) {$\\$1{,}000{,}000$};\n\t\\node[font=\\bfseries] at (8.0,3.5) {Option 2};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r2) at (6.8,2.0) {2};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (9.4,3.2) node[lpr, midway, above] {$0.10$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (9.4,2.0) node[lpr, midway, above] {$0.89$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (9.4,0.8) node[lpr, midway, below] {$0.01$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.5,3.2) {$\\$5{,}000{,}000$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.5,2.0) {$\\$1{,}000{,}000$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.5,0.8) {$\\$0$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Option 1 and Option 2.}\n\\label{fig:allaisone}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b9.1.3": "Then, Option 3: $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ with $0.11$, else $\\$0$. Option 4: $\\$5{,}000{,}000$ with $0.10$, else $\\$0$.",
 "b9.1.4": "\\begin{figure}[H]\n\\centering\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]\n\t\\node[font=\\bfseries] at (1.6,3.5) {Option 3};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r1) at (0.4,2.0) {1};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,2.8) node[lpr, midway, above] {$0.11$};\n\t\\draw (r1) -- (3.0,1.2) node[lpr, midway, below] {$0.89$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,2.8) {$\\$1{,}000{,}000$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (3.1,1.2) {$\\$0$};\n\t\\node[font=\\bfseries] at (8.0,3.5) {Option 4};\n\t\\node[cnode] (r2) at (6.8,2.0) {2};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (9.4,2.8) node[lpr, midway, above] {$0.10$};\n\t\\draw (r2) -- (9.4,1.2) node[lpr, midway, below] {$0.90$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.5,2.8) {$\\$5{,}000{,}000$};\n\t\\node[anchor=west] at (9.5,1.2) {$\\$0$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n\\caption{Option 3 and Option 4.}\n\\label{fig:allaistwo}\n\\end{figure}",
 "b9.1.5": "Most people prefer Option 1 over 2, and Option 4 over 3 (Allais, 1953). Savage himself answered that way at first. Thinking it over, he became convinced that the pattern was irrational, and changed his preferences.",
 "b9.1.6": "\\begin{center}\n\\small Write $Z$ for: $\\$5{,}000{,}000$ with probability $\\nicefrac{10}{11}$, else $\\$0$.\n\\end{center}",
 "b9.1.7": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}l|cc@{}}\n\\toprule\n         & with probability $0.11$ & with probability $0.89$ \\\\ \\midrule\nOption 1 & $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ & $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ \\\\\nOption 2 & $Z$               & $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ \\\\ \\midrule\nOption 3 & $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ & $\\$0$ \\\\\nOption 4 & $Z$               & $\\$0$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b9.1.8": "Within each pair the second column is shared. Writing $M$ for $\\$1{,}000{,}000$ and $O$ for $\\$0$: Option 1 $= \\mix{M}{0.11}{M}$, Option 2 $= \\mix{Z}{0.11}{M}$, Option 3 $= \\mix{M}{0.11}{O}$, Option 4 $= \\mix{Z}{0.11}{O}$. By Independence, Option 1 $\\succsim$ Option 2 iff $M \\succsim Z$ iff Option 3 $\\succsim$ Option 4. So Option 1 $\\succ$ Option 2 together with Option 4 $\\succ$ Option 3 violates Independence.",
 "h9.2": "\\subsection{Ellsberg's urn}",
 "b9.2.1": "\\begin{center}\n\\small $30$ red balls, and $60$ that are black or yellow in an unknown proportion. One ball is drawn.\n\\end{center}",
 "b9.2.2": "\\begin{center}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}l|ccc@{}}\n\\toprule\n          & red ($30$) & black ($?$) & yellow ($?$) \\\\ \\midrule\nBet I     & $\\$100$ & $\\$0$   & $\\$0$   \\\\\nBet II    & $\\$0$   & $\\$100$ & $\\$0$   \\\\ \\midrule\nBet III   & $\\$100$ & $\\$0$   & $\\$100$ \\\\\nBet IV    & $\\$0$   & $\\$100$ & $\\$100$ \\\\ \\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}",
 "b9.2.3": "Most people have I $\\succ$ II, and IV $\\succ$ III. Within each pair the yellow column is shared, and the sure-thing principle says I $\\succsim$ II iff III $\\succsim$ IV. No probabilistic credence in ``black'' makes both preferences expected-utility maximising.",
 "h9.3": "\\subsection{Responses}",
 "b9.3.1": "\\begin{itemize}\n\t\\item \\textbf{Bite the bullet:} the pattern is a mistake, and on reflection people revise it.\n\t\\item \\textbf{Redescribe the outcomes:} $\\$0$ after turning down a sure million is not the same outcome as $\\$0$. Then Independence isn't violated, but it becomes hard to violate anything.\n\t\\item \\textbf{Give up Independence:} theories that weight probabilities by rank, or by the agent's attitude to risk.\n\\end{itemize}",
 "h9": "\\section{Are the axioms requirements of rationality?}"
};
